%!PS-Adobe-3.0
%%Title: halftonekit.ps — reusable halftone screens and misregistration offsets
%%Creator: pscat — issue #53
%%EndComments

% A sibling to hatchkit.ps/stipplekit.ps (issues #49/#50): one operator,
% `halftone`, that fills whatever region is currently clipped with a
% regular print screen — dot, line, or cross-line — sized per cell by a
% tone, with a per-layer registration offset for controlled
% misregistration. Screen print, risograph, and comic-book halftones
% are all the same underlying construction (a lattice of marks whose
% size tracks a tone field, layered a few times with small offsets);
% this is that construction, not a rendering pipeline of its own — the
% caller supplies the region (via an ordinary `clip`), the tone (a
% number or a `{x y -> w}` callback), and the layering (one call per
% ink, each with its own color, angle, and offset).
%
%   newpath 40 40 moveto 200 40 lineto 200 200 lineto 40 200 lineto closepath clip
%   << /Screen /dot /Frequency 9 /Tone { 100 div exch pop } >> halftone
%
% `halftone` does not clip on its own. Set the region you want screened
% as the current clip *before* calling it — `<path> clip` — the same
% way `hatch` works. The lattice is swept across /BBox (below) and
% relies entirely on the graphics state's real clip to cut it to
% shape, so it screens concave and self-intersecting paths exactly as
% well as convex ones, with no point-in-polygon code anywhere in this
% file. This also sidesteps `clippath`'s known limitation (issue #120:
% it reports only the newest of several nested clips, not their true
% intersection) — `halftone` never calls `clippath` at all.
%
% Deliberately NOT built on artkit.ps's `scatter`, though stipplekit.ps
% is a thin layer over exactly that: `scatter` is a random,
% retry-until-accepted placement engine, and a halftone is a
% deterministic lattice — a different engine, not a differently
% parameterized one. Routing a fixed grid through a random placer
% would add noise the medium is defined by not having, and would
% need a /Seed where none is wanted: this operator makes zero random
% draws, so fixed options reproduce identical output in any
% interpreter, with no stream to seed or restore. A /Tone callback
% that calls `rand` itself still works, under the caller's own `srand`.
%
% Options:
%   /BBox [x0 y0 x1 y1]  the area to sweep the lattice across. If
%           omitted, read from `pathbbox` — the *current path's*
%           bounding box — so the usual call shape is
%           `<path> clip << ... >> halftone` (`clip` does not consume
%           the current path, so it is still there for `pathbbox` to
%           read; `halftone` itself brackets its drawing in `gsave`/
%           `grestore`, so the path survives a call too — a second,
%           layered call over the same clip, the classic misregistered
%           second plate, can keep relying on the default). A caller
%           who clips from a path they no longer hold must pass /BBox
%           explicitly. Either way the box only decides which lattice
%           cells get visited — the real clip alone decides what
%           actually inks the page, so a loose /BBox costs a few wasted
%           cells, never wrong output. Converse for /Offset users: the
%           lattice is swept across this box and shifted afterward, so
%           a tight box plus a nonzero shift leaves an un-inked edge
%           strip up to the shift width — inflate /BBox when shifting.
%           Must be an array of exactly four numbers
%           (`halftone-bbox-must-be-an-array-of-four-numbers` for a
%           non-array or non-numeric elements — a 4-long string would
%           otherwise silently reinterpret as byte values — and
%           `halftone-bbox-must-have-four-elements` for any other
%           length, since an oversized array would otherwise litter
%           the operand stack past what `opts halftone -` promises)
%           and non-degenerate (`halftone-bbox-degenerate`).
%   /Screen /dot, /line, or /cross (default /dot — anything not
%           `eq` to one of the three is
%           `halftone-screen-must-be-dot-line-or-cross`). Comparison is
%           the language's own `eq`, so a string with the same
%           characters (`(dot)`) selects the same screen as the name —
%           verified identical in Ghostscript, not a pscat quirk. A dot screen
%           draws one filled circle per cell; a line screen draws one
%           round-capped segment per cell along /Angle, each exactly
%           one pitch long so neighbors join into continuous rules at
%           uniform tone; a cross screen draws the line pass twice in
%           one call — at /Angle, then at /Angle + 90 over the *same*
%           lattice origin, so the crosses stay centered on their
%           cells. A multi-color cross (each direction its own ink) is
%           two caller-side calls instead; both shapes compose, the
%           same way layered `hatch` calls do.
%   /Frequency num > 0  cells per inch, the print-native unit (lpi) —
%           the issue's own vocabulary ("configurable angle,
%           frequency, and mark size"), not a second name for a pitch
%           (default 9, i.e. an 8-unit cell). The pitch is
%           72/Frequency user units: under a scaled CTM the lattice
%           scales with user space like everything else, the same
%           caveat hatchkit's /Spacing already carries. Validated
%           (`halftone-frequency-must-be-a-number` /
%           `halftone-frequency-must-be-positive`).
%   /Angle num  screen angle in degrees (default 45, print's own black-
%           plate angle). Any number; validated only to *be* a number
%           (`halftone-angle-must-be-a-number`). Per-layer rotation
%           offsets — the issue's "translation/rotation offsets" — are
%           just per-call /Angle values; no separate option exists for
%           what a second call already expresses.
%   /Tone num or {x y -> w}  the ink per cell: a plain number for a
%           flat screen, or a procedure sampled once per cell center
%           for procedural tone values (a gradient, a function plot, a
%           radial vignette — the caller supplies the field, image
%           analysis stays out of scope). The sample point is in the
%           same pre-offset user-space coordinates as /BBox, so a
%           /Tone field stays registered to the page while /Offset
%           shifts the lattice — misregistration moves the *plate*,
%           not the image, which is the whole point. The result is
%           clamped into [0,1] *before* any size is derived from it,
%           so a wild return changes the tone, never the geometry —
%           and never reaches `sqrt` unclamped (a negative input is a
%           domain error in both this interpreter and Ghostscript). A
%           constant is clamped once up front; anything neither numeric
%           nor a procedure is
%           `halftone-tone-must-be-a-number-or-procedure`, reported
%           before the value is ever executed. The proc MUST consume
%           both operands and leave exactly one number, and must not
%           touch any `hf` name (see "Scratch prefix" below). The
%           consume-both-leave-one-number half is enforced per sample,
%           not just documented: a proc that leaves anything else gets
%           `halftone-tone-proc-must-leave-exactly-one-result`, and a
%           non-numeric single result gets
%           `halftone-tone-must-return-a-number` — both unwind via
%           counted operand-stack pops plus `grestore`, so even these
%           exits leave a `stopped`-recovering caller exactly as found
%           (a `mark` sentinel would do until a callback forged a mark
%           of its own and shadowed `cleartomark`). (Enforcement
%           matters here beyond hygiene: the walk
%           reads its dispatch state through fixed-depth `index`, so a
%           leaked operand would shift every read and fail deep in the
%           walk under an internal name.) A proc that fails mid-call
%           in any other way (typecheck, stackunderflow, its own
%           error) unwinds past the call's `grestore` — treat that as
%           fatal, the same convention hatchkit's /Density carries,
%           not something to catch with `stopped` and continue from. A sample
%           at or below 0 draws no mark at all — note this is load-
%           bearing, not just an optimization: a zero-width stroke in
%           PostScript is a hairline, not nothing, so the line/cross
%           branches must skip rather than draw through zero.
%   /MaxRadius num > 0  the dot radius at full tone (default half the
%           cell pitch, so full-tone dots exactly touch). Only read on
%           the /dot branch — validated there
%           (`halftone-maxradius-must-be-a-number` /
%           `halftone-maxradius-must-be-positive`), merely harmless
%           elsewhere, so a shared options dict carrying a stale or
%           exploratory /MaxRadius alongside a line screen is not
%           penalized for an option the call never touches.
%   /MaxWidth num > 0  the line width at full tone (default a full
%           pitch, so full-tone rules exactly merge). Only read on the
%           /line and /cross branches, same "harmless elsewhere" shape
%           as /MaxRadius.
%   /Offset [dx dy]  per-layer registration shift in user units
%           (default [0 0]), applied with `translate` inside the call's
%           own `gsave`/`grestore`, unconditionally — `translate 0 0`
%           adds exactly 0.0, so "disabled" and "omitted" are the same
%           operation, not two paths that must be kept agreeing. A
%           risograph two-plate spread is two calls differing only in
%           color and /Offset. Must be a two-element array of numbers
%           (`halftone-offset-must-be-a-two-element-array` otherwise —
%           checked *before* unpacking, so an oversized array cannot
%           litter the stack the way an unchecked `aload pop` would).
%   /MaxCells n  hard cap on visited lattice cells (default 200000),
%           checked *before* any drawing or /Tone call. One budget is
%           enough here, and deliberately the only one: exactly one
%           /Tone sample happens per cell and each cell draws a fixed
%           one mark (dot/line) or two (cross) known up front, so the
%           cell count bounds callbacks, geometry, and ink uniformly —
%           the two-budget shape hatchkit needs exists only because its
%           per-line sample counts vary. Must be a number >= 1
%           (`halftone-maxcells-must-be-a-number` /
%           `halftone-maxcells-must-be-positive`); exceeding it is
%           `halftone-maxcells-exceeded`, with nothing drawn.
%
% Size mapping: dot radius = /MaxRadius * sqrt(tone) — area-
% proportional, the print-correct curve (ink coverage grows with the
% dot's area, not its radius); line width = /MaxWidth * tone —
% linear, since a rule's coverage grows with its width. Documented
% here rather than merely implemented so the next reader doesn't
% "simplify" the sqrt away.
%
% Reproducibility: no random draws anywhere in this file, so none of
% the seeded-stream caveats the rand-driven siblings document apply —
% the same options draw the same marks in this interpreter and in
% Ghostscript alike, modulo each rasterizer's own arc rendering. The
% caller's ambient `rand` stream is never touched.
%
% Layering recipe (the issue's "spot-color layering" and
% "misregistration", caller-side): set the ink with `setrgbcolor` (or
% whatever color operator the job uses), `clip` once, then one
% `halftone` call per plate, varying /Offset by a few units and
% usually /Angle and /Tone per plate too. `halftone` never sets the
% color itself — like `hatch`, it inks in the current color.
%
% Scratch prefix: every internal helper and working-state name starts
% with `hf` — checked against every sibling before picking it
% (artkit's hyperbolic driver owns `ht*`, handscript owns `hs-*`,
% hatchkit owns `h*`/`hk*`). /Tone runs mid-loop with the same two
% obligations `scatter`'s /Mark and /Weight document: it MUST consume
% both operands (a leaking proc shows up on the operand stack, which
% the test harness asserts empty after every call), and it must not
% touch any `hf` name. Two tiers, mirroring hatchkit's own contract:
% the dispatch kind, the const/proc choice, the lattice shape, and
% the pass count travel on the operand stack *under* the whole walk,
% read back only through fixed-depth `index` — a dict redefinition
% mid-call cannot reach them, so a /Tone proc cannot make a call
% exceed what /MaxCells approved no matter what it redefines. The
% walk is cell-major: one flat loop over cells, sampling once per
% cell, with an inner pass loop (one trip, two for cross) whose limit
% re-reads the npass *snapshot* per cell — safe, because a stack
% value cannot be redefined, unlike a dict limit re-read per row.
% Every other `hf` name only affects geometry or tone, so corrupting
% one is undefined rendering, not a safety-limit bypass.
%
% Ghostscript: core Level 1/2 operators only (`arc`, `cos`, `sin`,
% `sqrt`, `translate`, `pathbbox`, `xcheck`, `forall`/`for`) — the
% specimen page runs unchanged under `gs` (pinned by
% `ghostscript_accepts_the_halftone_specimen_sheet` in
% tests/halftonekit.rs, the same shape as every sibling's).

% dict key default hfgetdef -> value
% The same wrap-the-default-in-an-array trick as hatchkit's hkgetdef /
% artkit's scgetdef / paintkit's pkgetdef / pagekit's pggetdef: a name
% bound directly to a procedure auto-executes on bare reference, so
% the default travels inside a 1-element array instead.
% @internal
/hfgetdef {
  /hfgdd exch 1 array astore def
  /hfgdk exch def
  /hfgdt exch def
  hfgdt hfgdk known { hfgdt hfgdk get } { hfgdd 0 get } ifelse
} def

% v hfisnum -> bool, true for integers and reals alike
% @internal
/hfisnum {
  dup type /integertype eq exch type /realtype eq or
} def

% v hfclamp01 -> v', clamped into [0,1]
% @internal
/hfclamp01 {
  dup 0 lt { pop 0 } if
  dup 1 gt { pop 1 } if
} def

% v hfcallable -> bool, true only for an executable array
% Deliberately narrower than a bare `xcheck`: `xcheck` is also true
% for a `cvx`-marked string or an executable name, which are not
% things this file can `exec` with {x y -> w} calling convention —
% accepting them would misroute a malformed /Tone to the drawing pass
% instead of the validation error (stipplekit's round-3 lesson). A
% caller holding a procedure under a name passes it loaded
% (`/MyTone load`), which is already the plain executable array this
% accepts; a bare executable name is rejected, documented in /Tone.
% @internal
/hfcallable {
  dup type /arraytype eq exch xcheck and
} def

% hfcleanup -
% Pops the operand stack back to the recorded entry depth (clamped
% at zero — a callback that over-consumed pushes the count
% negative, and `repeat` must never see that), then restores the one
% graphics save this call holds. Every per-sample contract exit runs
% this before signaling, so a `stopped`-recovering caller resumes
% with both stacks exactly as found. Positional on purpose: a `mark`
% sentinel would work until a callback forges a mark of its own and
% shadows `cleartomark`, stranding the snapshots — counted pops have
% nothing to shadow.
% @internal
/hfcleanup {
  count hfdepth0 sub dup 0 lt { pop 0 } if { pop } repeat
  grestore
} def

% opts halftone -
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Fills the current clip region with a regular print screen — dot, line, or cross-line — with per-cell tone-driven mark sizing and a per-layer registration offset. Set the region with <path> clip first; layer one call per ink for misregistered multi-plate work.
% @example: newpath 40 40 moveto 200 40 lineto 200 200 lineto 40 200 lineto closepath clip << /Screen /dot /Frequency 9 /Tone 0.5 >> halftone
% @param: /BBox [x0 y0 x1 y1] area to sweep the lattice across; defaults to pathbbox of the current path; must be an array of exactly four numbers
% @param: /Screen /dot, /line, or /cross — one filled circle, one round-capped rule segment, or two perpendicular rule segments per cell (default /dot)
% @param: /Frequency num > 0 cells per inch (default 9)
% @param: /Angle num screen angle in degrees; per-layer rotation offsets are per-call values (default 45)
% @param: /Tone num or {x y -> w} flat ink or a per-cell-center tone callback in pre-offset user-space coordinates, clamped to [0,1] (default 1)
% @param: /MaxRadius num > 0 dot radius at full tone, default half the cell pitch; read only for /dot, harmless otherwise
% @param: /MaxWidth num > 0 rule width at full tone, default a full pitch; read only for /line and /cross, harmless otherwise
% @param: /Offset [dx dy] per-layer registration shift in user units, applied unconditionally via translate (default [0 0])
% @param: /MaxCells n hard cap on visited lattice cells, checked before any drawing or /Tone call (default 200000)
/halftone {
  % The options operand arrives wrapped: a caller who passes a bare
  % procedure where the dict belongs must get
  % halftone-opts-must-be-a-dict, not an execution of their proc off
  % a bare reference (stipplekit's round-2 lesson — `get` never
  % executes, a bare name bound to a proc always does).
  1 array astore /hfoptsbox exch def
  hfoptsbox 0 get type /dicttype ne { halftone-opts-must-be-a-dict } if
  /hfopts hfoptsbox 0 get def

  hfopts /BBox known
    {
      % Boxed, like every other caller-supplied value in this file:
      % the elements are read out through `get` (which never
      % executes) and only bound to bare names after proving numeric
      % — a procedure smuggled in as an element must fail validation,
      % never run off a bare reference in the arithmetic below.
      hfopts /BBox get 1 array astore /hfbbox exch def
      hfbbox 0 get type /arraytype ne { halftone-bbox-must-be-an-array-of-four-numbers } if
      % Length first: `aload` unpacks every element, not just four,
      % and an oversized array would otherwise litter the operand
      % stack past what `opts halftone -` promises (hatchkit's
      % round-3 lesson). Reading through the box leaves nothing
      % behind on any path, so the error exits are clean too.
      hfbbox 0 get length 4 ne { halftone-bbox-must-have-four-elements } if
      hfbbox 0 get 0 get hfisnum not { halftone-bbox-must-be-an-array-of-four-numbers } if
      hfbbox 0 get 1 get hfisnum not { halftone-bbox-must-be-an-array-of-four-numbers } if
      hfbbox 0 get 2 get hfisnum not { halftone-bbox-must-be-an-array-of-four-numbers } if
      hfbbox 0 get 3 get hfisnum not { halftone-bbox-must-be-an-array-of-four-numbers } if
      hfbbox 0 get 0 get /hfbx0 exch def
      hfbbox 0 get 1 get /hfby0 exch def
      hfbbox 0 get 2 get /hfbx1 exch def
      hfbbox 0 get 3 get /hfby1 exch def
    }
    { pathbbox /hfby1 exch def /hfbx1 exch def /hfby0 exch def /hfbx0 exch def }
  ifelse
  hfbx1 hfbx0 le hfby1 hfby0 le or { halftone-bbox-degenerate } if

  % /Screen collapses to a small int immediately: the value arrives
  % boxed because a proc-typed /Screen must compare, never execute,
  % and an int can never auto-execute no matter what the caller
  % passed. 0 = dot, 1 = line, 2 = cross.
  hfopts /Screen /dot hfgetdef 1 array astore /hfscreenbox exch def
  hfscreenbox 0 get /dot eq { /hfscreenkind 0 def }
  { hfscreenbox 0 get /line eq { /hfscreenkind 1 def }
  { hfscreenbox 0 get /cross eq { /hfscreenkind 2 def }
  { halftone-screen-must-be-dot-line-or-cross } ifelse } ifelse } ifelse

  hfopts /Frequency 9 hfgetdef 1 array astore /hffreqbox exch def
  hffreqbox 0 get hfisnum not { halftone-frequency-must-be-a-number } if
  hffreqbox 0 get 0 le { halftone-frequency-must-be-positive } if
  /hffreq hffreqbox 0 get def
  /hfpitch 72 hffreq div def

  hfopts /Angle 45 hfgetdef 1 array astore /hfangbox exch def
  hfangbox 0 get hfisnum not { halftone-angle-must-be-a-number } if
  /hfang hfangbox 0 get def
  hfang cos /hfdx exch def
  hfang sin /hfdy exch def
  % The normal is derived algebraically from the direction — a
  % +90-degree rotation is exactly (-dy, dx) — never via an
  % independent cos/sin(angle+90): two separate trig evaluations of
  % different inputs are not guaranteed exactly orthogonal in floating
  % point (hatchkit's round-1 lesson), and the cross screen's second
  % pass reuses this same normal as its direction, orthogonal to the
  % first pass by construction.
  hfdy neg /hfnx exch def
  hfdx /hfny exch def

  % Constant /Tone clamps once here; a callback's returns clamp per
  % sample, right after `exec`, before `sqrt` can ever see a
  % negative (a sqrt domain error in both interpreters).
  hfopts /Tone 1 hfgetdef 1 array astore /hftonebox exch def
  hftonebox 0 get hfisnum
    { /hftoneconst true def /hftoneval hftonebox 0 get hfclamp01 def }
    { hftonebox 0 get hfcallable not { halftone-tone-must-be-a-number-or-procedure } if
      /hftoneconst false def }
  ifelse

  % Branch-conditional size options: only the branch that draws reads
  % its knob, so an unused-but-malformed one in a shared dict is
  % harmless, never an error (stipplekit's /DotRadius lesson).
  hfscreenkind 0 eq
    {
      hfopts /MaxRadius known
        { hfopts /MaxRadius get 1 array astore /hfmrbox exch def
          hfmrbox 0 get hfisnum not { halftone-maxradius-must-be-a-number } if
          hfmrbox 0 get 0 le { halftone-maxradius-must-be-positive } if
          /hfmaxr hfmrbox 0 get def }
        { /hfmaxr hfpitch 2 div def }
      ifelse
    }
    {
      hfopts /MaxWidth known
        { hfopts /MaxWidth get 1 array astore /hfmwbox exch def
          hfmwbox 0 get hfisnum not { halftone-maxwidth-must-be-a-number } if
          hfmwbox 0 get 0 le { halftone-maxwidth-must-be-positive } if
          /hfmaxw hfmwbox 0 get def }
        { /hfmaxw hfpitch def }
      ifelse
    }
  ifelse

  % Read and validated element by element through boxes — never an
  % `aload pop`, whose oversized-array litter is exactly what the
  % /BBox length check above exists to prevent.
  hfopts /Offset known { hfopts /Offset get } { [ 0 0 ] } ifelse
  1 array astore /hfoffbox exch def
  hfoffbox 0 get type /arraytype ne { halftone-offset-must-be-a-two-element-array } if
  hfoffbox 0 get length 2 ne { halftone-offset-must-be-a-two-element-array } if
  hfoffbox 0 get 0 get 1 array astore /hfoxbox exch def
  hfoxbox 0 get hfisnum not { halftone-offset-must-be-a-two-element-array } if
  hfoffbox 0 get 1 get 1 array astore /hfoybox exch def
  hfoybox 0 get hfisnum not { halftone-offset-must-be-a-two-element-array } if
  /hfox hfoxbox 0 get def
  /hfoy hfoybox 0 get def

  hfopts /MaxCells 200000 hfgetdef 1 array astore /hfmcbox exch def
  hfmcbox 0 get hfisnum not { halftone-maxcells-must-be-a-number } if
  hfmcbox 0 get 1 lt { halftone-maxcells-must-be-positive } if
  /hfmaxcells hfmcbox 0 get def

  % ---- lattice geometry, computed ONCE and stored ----
  % The four /BBox corners are projected onto the screen direction
  % and its normal; the lattice spans both ranges at one pitch step.
  % These stored counts and origins feed the pre-flight check AND
  % every drawing loop below — nothing is recomputed per pass, so
  % the budget's arithmetic and the loops' actual trip counts agree
  % by construction, not by parallel formulas that can drift apart
  % in floating point (hatchkit's round-4 lesson: two computations
  % expected to agree, evaluated on far-from-origin coordinates,
  % genuinely disagreed by a whole candidate).
  [ hfbx0 hfby0 hfbx1 hfby0 hfbx0 hfby1 hfbx1 hfby1 ]
  /hfcorners exch def
  /hfddmin 1.0e30 def
  /hfddmax -1.0e30 def
  /hfkkmin 1.0e30 def
  /hfkkmax -1.0e30 def
  0 2 6 {
    /hfk exch def
    hfcorners hfk get /hfcx exch def
    hfcorners hfk 1 add get /hfcy exch def
    hfcx hfdx mul hfcy hfdy mul add /hfdd exch def
    hfcx hfnx mul hfcy hfny mul add /hfkk exch def
    hfdd hfddmin lt { /hfddmin hfdd def } if
    hfdd hfddmax gt { /hfddmax hfdd def } if
    hfkk hfkkmin lt { /hfkkmin hfkk def } if
    hfkk hfkkmax gt { /hfkkmax hfkk def } if
  } for
  hfddmax hfddmin sub hfpitch div cvi 1 add /hfncols exch def
  hfkkmax hfkkmin sub hfpitch div cvi 1 add /hfnrows exch def

  % ---- deterministic pre-flight budget check (no drawing, no /Tone call) ----
  % One /Tone sample per cell and a fixed one mark (dot/line) or two
  % (cross) per cell, so this single count bounds callbacks,
  % geometry, and ink together.
  hfncols hfnrows mul hfmaxcells gt { halftone-maxcells-exceeded } if

  % gsave/grestore, not just a bare setlinewidth/setlinecap: the
  % line/cross branches set both per mark, which would otherwise
  % silently overwrite the caller's graphics state with no way back.
  % It also protects the current path, so a second, layered call over
  % the same clip keeps defaulting /BBox from `pathbbox`.
  gsave
  % Unconditional: `translate 0 0` adds exactly 0.0, so an omitted
  % /Offset and an explicit [0 0] take the same path — one code path,
  % no zero/nonzero branches to keep agreeing.
  hfox hfoy translate
  1 setlinecap
  % The cross screen's two passes share one lattice origin and one
  % tone sample per cell — only the segment direction swaps — so its
  % crosses stay centered and both arms agree even for a stateful
  % /Tone. (Sampling per pass instead would run a callback twice per
  % cell with doubled side effects, contradicting the documented
  % one-sample-per-cell contract the /MaxCells premise is built on.)
  /hfcells hfncols hfnrows mul def
  % Six snapshots live on the operand stack under the whole walk,
  % bottom to top: kind const val cols rows npass. `for` consumes
  % only its own limit/proc/initial (pushed after these) and a
  % well-behaved /Tone leaves the stack exactly as it found it, so
  % fixed-depth `index` reads below can never observe a dict
  % redefinition — the dispatch kind, the const/proc choice, the
  % lattice shape, and the pass count are immune to mid-call
  % tampering by construction, not by promise. (History, newest
  % first: the flat pass-major loop sampled /Tone once per *trip*,
  % i.e. twice per cross cell; the nested loops before it re-read a
  % dict limit per row, so redefining it multiplied every remaining
  % row past the approved budget. Cell-major with a stack-read inner
  % pass loop closes both; redefinition now corrupts placement at
  % worst — tier-2 geometry, per the header's own tiering — never
  % the trip count.)
  % No mark sentinel under the snapshots: a callback can forge a mark
  % of its own and shadow `cleartomark`, stranding the snapshots on a
  % `stopped` caller's stack. Positional cleanup cannot be shadowed,
  % so the entry depth is recorded instead and every contract exit
  % pops exactly back to it — then `grestore`, so graphics state is
  % restored too — before signaling. The success path pops all six.
  count /hfdepth0 exch def
  hfscreenkind hftoneconst hftoneconst { hftoneval } { 0 } ifelse hfncols hfnrows
  hfscreenkind 2 eq { 2 } { 1 } ifelse
  0 1 hfcells 1 sub {
    /hfcell exch def
    % Per-cell copies from the immune snapshots: everything below is
    % derived before /Tone runs, so a hostile callback redefining
    % the dict originals changes where this cell inks, never how
    % many cells run.
    2 index /hfnc exch def
    1 index /hfnr exch def
    /hfj hfcell hfnc idiv def
    /hfi hfcell hfnc mod def
    /hfkk hfkkmin hfj hfpitch mul add def
    /hfdd hfddmin hfi hfpitch mul add def
    /hfcx hfdd hfdx mul hfkk hfnx mul add def
    /hfcy hfdd hfdy mul hfkk hfny mul add def
    4 index
      { 3 index }
      { % The snapshots above make a stack-imbalanced /Tone actively
        % confusing rather than merely lint-visible: leaked operands
        % would shift every `index` read below, failing deep in the
        % walk under an internal name. So the contract (consume both,
        % leave exactly one number) is enforced per sample, not just
        % documented — with errors that name the contract and unwind
        % stack- *and* graphics-clean (counted pops plus `grestore`),
        % so even these exits leave a `stopped`-recovering caller
        % exactly as found.
        % E (kept on the stack through `exec`) is the pre-call
        % count minus one, so post-call M satisfies M - E = 1 iff the
        % proc left exactly one result: E itself is still counted in M.
        hfcx hfcy count 1 sub 3 1 roll
        hftonebox 0 get exec
        count 2 index sub 1 ne
          { hfcleanup halftone-tone-proc-must-leave-exactly-one-result } if
        exch pop
        dup hfisnum not
          { hfcleanup halftone-tone-must-return-a-number } if
        hfclamp01 }
    ifelse
    /hfw exch def
    % The inner pass limit re-reads the npass *snapshot* per cell —
    % safe, because a stack value cannot be redefined, unlike the
    % dict limit the pre-flat-loop version re-read per row.
    0 1 2 index 1 sub {
      /hfpass exch def
      hfpass 0 eq
        { /hfpdx hfdx def /hfpdy hfdy def }
        { /hfpdx hfnx def /hfpdy hfny def }
      ifelse
      % Zero tone draws no mark: skipped, never drawn through —
      % a zero-width stroke would render as a hairline, not as
      % nothing, so this branch is correctness, not optimization.
      hfw 0 gt {
        5 index 0 eq {
          % Area-proportional dots (see the header for why sqrt).
          % Per-mark newpath: without it every cell's arc would
          % accumulate into one giant path (memory, time) and a
          % leftover caller path would fill on first contact.
          newpath hfcx hfcy hfmaxr hfw sqrt mul 0 360 arc fill
        } {
          % One pitch-long rule centered on the cell along the
          % pass direction: neighbors join into continuous lines
          % at uniform tone, round caps hiding the seams.
          hfmaxw hfw mul setlinewidth
          newpath
          hfcx hfpdx hfpitch 2 div mul sub
          hfcy hfpdy hfpitch 2 div mul sub
          moveto
          hfcx hfpdx hfpitch 2 div mul add
          hfcy hfpdy hfpitch 2 div mul add
          lineto stroke
        } ifelse
      } if
    } for
  } for
  pop pop pop pop pop pop
  grestore
} def
